"comparison": "Both Morgan Stanley and Citi disclose valuation and credit loss considerations for margin loans. Morgan Stanley calculates the fair value of equity margin loans by discounting future interest cash flows, explicitly netting out potential losses modeled using market observable metrics like CDS spreads and volatility to account for downward price movements of the collateral. Citi similarly addresses margin loans by determining Allowances for Credit Losses (ACL) based on the excess of the asset's amortized cost basis over the fair value of the collateral, noting that no ACL is recorded if the collateral value equals or exceeds the cost basis. While Morgan Stanley's disclosure focuses on the fair value measurement technique including specific modeling inputs for loss calibration, Citi's disclosure focuses on the credit loss allowance recognition threshold relative to collateral fair value; both highlight the critical role of collateral fair value and credit risk metrics (CDS) in assessing the risk associated with margin lending.",
